<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nakhchivan Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic was an autonomous republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> SSR, created in 1924 for the Nakhchivan region, separated from the rest of Azerbaijan by Armenian territory. Its status followed the treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> of 1921, which placed it under Azerbaijan. In 1990 it became the Nakhchivan Autonomous Republic, which it remains within independent Azerbaijan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakhchivan-autonomous-soviet-socialist-republic.jpg" alt="Nakhchivan Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ericmetro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Nakhichevan_ASSR_(1978%E2%80%931990).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the last years of the First World War Nakhichevan saw bloodshed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and Azerbaijanis, who both claimed the area. By 1914 the Armenian population had fallen slightly to 40 per cent, while the Azeri population had risen to roughly 60 per cent. After the dissolution of the Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic in May 1918, the region was contested between the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> occupied it in June 1918 and massacred 10,000 Armenians. In December 1918 Jafargulu Khan Nakhichevanski declared a Republic of Aras, which led to the Aras War. By mid-June 1919 Armenia held the territory, but an Azerbaijani invasion followed and further violence left some 10,000 Armenians dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied the region in July 1920 and on 28 July declared an autonomous Soviet republic with close ties to the Azerbaijan SSR. In November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, but Lenin called for a referendum, which at the start of 1921 showed 90 per cent in favour of inclusion in the Azerbaijan SSR as an autonomous republic. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> between Bolshevik Russia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> of 16 March 1921 settled the matter, adding the Sharur-Daralagez district so that Turkey shared a border with Azerbaijan, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> of 23 October reaffirmed it. On 9 February 1924 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> placed the republic under the jurisdiction of the Azerbaijan SSR, and its constitution was adopted on 18 April 1926.</p><p>Under Soviet rule the republic became a centre of industry, notably the mining of salt, and a junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> railways. Public health improved: malaria, which had infected between 70 and 85 per cent of the population, was sharply reduced, and trachoma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> were eliminated. The Armenian share of the population fell from 15 per cent in 1926 to 1.4 per cent in 1979, while the Azeri share rose from 85 to 96 per cent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In December 1989 Azeri inhabitants moved to dismantle the Soviet border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In January 1990 the Supreme Soviet of the republic declared an intention to secede from the USSR, in protest at the Soviet action in Black January, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s declaration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heydar-aliyev">Heydar Aliyev</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a>, returned there in autumn 1990 and was elected to the local Supreme Soviet. On 19 November 1990 the republic became the Nakhichevan Autonomous Republic within the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nakhichevan Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic included the Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast and the <b>Nakhchivan ASSR</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-ssr">Azerbaijan SSR</a></li></ul></section>
